1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,647
The cleanup was done properly. A fresh
mirror clone, every ref rewritten, the scan

2
00:00:09,687 --> 00:00:11,924
clean, a forced push after a freeze.

3
00:00:12,264 --> 00:00:16,301
The next morning the scanner reports
the same secret on main again.

4
00:00:16,341 --> 00:00:20,744
The branch rule that forbids force
pushes was back on and it didn't fire.

5
00:00:20,784 --> 00:00:25,673
Nobody did anything unusual. One colleague
ran git pull and git push, as on every

6
00:00:25,713 --> 00:00:26,525
other morning.

7
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:31,460
Today you reconstruct from the commit graph
how that happens, you learn the exact

8
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,922
instruction to give colleagues about
their clones, and you see what remains on

9
00:00:35,962 --> 00:00:40,385
GitHub that no Git command of yours can
reach. Hold on to one detail: the rule

10
00:00:40,425 --> 00:00:44,733
against force pushes didn't fire. One
line of push output will show you why.

11
00:00:46,573 --> 00:00:50,409
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,449 --> 00:00:55,238
Three words first. A commit is one saved
snapshot of the project, with an ID

13
00:00:55,278 --> 00:01:00,066
computed from its content. A clone is
a copy of a repository on one machine.

14
00:01:00,106 --> 00:01:05,022
And a stale clone is a clone that still
has the history from before a rewrite.

15
00:01:05,362 --> 00:01:09,414
In video 167 the rewrite ended with
the server holding clean refs.

16
00:01:09,454 --> 00:01:13,631
A ref is a name, such as a branch
or a tag, that points at a commit.

17
00:01:13,671 --> 00:01:17,475
That's the end of the command
and the middle of the operation.

18
00:01:17,515 --> 00:01:22,683
Two things remain. Other people's clones
still contain the complete old history, and

19
00:01:22,723 --> 00:01:27,705
Git sees nothing wrong with that history.
And on GitHub there are copies that you

20
00:01:27,745 --> 00:01:29,069
can't remove with Git.

21
00:01:29,409 --> 00:01:34,454
From video 59 you know what a rebased shared
branch does to the people who use it:

22
00:01:34,494 --> 00:01:39,602
their copy still descends from the old
commits. A whole-history rewrite is the same

23
00:01:39,642 --> 00:01:42,827
situation for every branch
and every person at once.

24
00:01:43,167 --> 00:01:46,431
The plan: first the failure,
replayed in a terminal.

25
00:01:46,471 --> 00:01:51,706
Then the three ways a stale clone can meet
the new history, and the one instruction

26
00:01:51,746 --> 00:01:56,790
that is safe to hand out. Then the rest
of the tabletop lab, from eradication to

27
00:01:56,830 --> 00:02:00,412
prevention, including the
failure repeated and recovered.

28
00:02:00,452 --> 00:02:05,432
Then the GitHub side, described from
GitHub's documentation, and the governance

29
00:02:05,472 --> 00:02:07,148
controls of section 21B.20.

30
00:02:08,988 --> 00:02:10,566
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:10,906 --> 00:02:12,318
After this video you can:

32
00:02:12,658 --> 00:02:17,386
Reconstruct from the commit graph how a
secret returns after a rewrite without any

33
00:02:17,426 --> 00:02:18,084
forced push.

34
00:02:18,424 --> 00:02:21,330
Tell collaborators exactly
what to do with their clones.

35
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:27,164
Explain what happens to a stale clone
under git pull --rebase and under a plain

36
00:02:27,204 --> 00:02:27,514
pull.

37
00:02:27,854 --> 00:02:32,667
Say what remains on GitHub after the forced
push and what a Support request covers,

38
00:02:32,707 --> 00:02:34,071
as the section gives it.

39
00:02:34,411 --> 00:02:38,769
Name the governance controls that limit
how far a leaked credential reaches.

40
00:02:40,609 --> 00:02:45,340
Why this failure exists. A rewrite produces
a second history that shares only its

41
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:50,170
oldest commits with the first. To Git, the
old history and the new history are two

42
00:02:50,210 --> 00:02:53,232
lines of work that happen
to have a common ancestor.

43
00:02:53,272 --> 00:02:57,886
Git has no concept of "the withdrawn
version". If someone merges the two lines,

44
00:02:57,926 --> 00:03:01,537
joining them in one new commit,
that's a merge like any other.

45
00:03:01,877 --> 00:03:06,737
What happens. A clone that still has the
old history merges or rebases it back and

46
00:03:06,777 --> 00:03:11,695
pushes it as ordinary new commits. The merge
commit has two parents: the clean tip,

47
00:03:11,735 --> 00:03:15,878
and a commit whose ancestors include
the commit that added the secret.

48
00:03:16,218 --> 00:03:20,814
The push from the clean tip to that merge
commit is a fast-forward: the server's

49
00:03:20,854 --> 00:03:25,393
current commit is an ancestor of the new
one, so the branch only moves forward.

50
00:03:25,433 --> 00:03:30,088
The server accepts it without force, and
no branch rule that forbids force pushes

51
00:03:30,128 --> 00:03:30,552
objects.

52
00:03:30,892 --> 00:03:36,018
How a clone is recovered. The filter-repo
manual says the easiest way to clean other

53
00:03:36,058 --> 00:03:40,939
clones is to delete and re-clone them.
Where that is impossible, it prescribes a

54
00:03:40,979 --> 00:03:42,108
sequence per clone.

55
00:03:42,448 --> 00:03:47,194
Six lines, and each has a reason. Tags
aren't updated by an ordinary fetch, so

56
00:03:47,234 --> 00:03:52,287
they're deleted and fetched again. A rebase
replays commits as new copies on top of

57
00:03:52,327 --> 00:03:56,583
another commit, and with --onto it
carries over only your own commits.

58
00:03:56,623 --> 00:04:00,510
The expiry and the prune remove
the old objects from this clone.

59
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:05,542
The last line is the verification: asking
for the type of the first changed commit

60
00:04:05,582 --> 00:04:06,156
must fail.

61
00:04:06,496 --> 00:04:11,316
The manual adds two warnings. Colleagues
must not run the same filter command

62
00:04:11,356 --> 00:04:16,239
themselves, because identical commands
can still produce different commit IDs.

63
00:04:16,279 --> 00:04:21,415
And the expiry step drops their reflogs
and stash entries, so they save their work

64
00:04:21,455 --> 00:04:21,794
first.

65
00:04:22,134 --> 00:04:27,067
GitHub's page lists "high risk of recontamination"
first among the side effects of a

66
00:04:27,107 --> 00:04:31,923
rewrite and gives the rule: collaborators
must rebase, not merge, branches created

67
00:04:31,963 --> 00:04:36,719
from the old history, because one merge
commit can reintroduce some or all of it.

68
00:04:37,059 --> 00:04:41,661
Can the server refuse the old commits?
Few hosting services can ban a specific

69
00:04:41,701 --> 00:04:46,423
commit from being pushed again. The
filter-repo manual says so, and the research

70
00:04:46,463 --> 00:04:51,244
notes of this course found no documented
built-in control of that kind on GitHub.

71
00:04:51,284 --> 00:04:56,006
Push protection, when the secret matches
a supported pattern, and a push ruleset

72
00:04:56,046 --> 00:04:59,756
that blocks the file path are the
nearest platform equivalents.

73
00:04:59,796 --> 00:05:04,339
The local analogue is a pre-receive hook,
a program the server runs before it

74
00:05:04,379 --> 00:05:07,256
accepts a push, which the
lab installs and tests.

75
00:05:07,596 --> 00:05:12,880
The GitHub side. This part is GitHub, not
Git, and it's described from GitHub's page

76
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:18,205
on removing sensitive data. Nothing here
was run. After your force push, four copies

77
00:05:18,245 --> 00:05:23,022
remain that you can't reach with Git.
A pull request is a GitHub object that

78
00:05:23,062 --> 00:05:28,219
proposes a merge, and a fork is a second
repository on GitHub made from the first.

79
00:05:28,559 --> 00:05:33,135
The Support request must contain the
repository owner and name, the number of

80
00:05:33,175 --> 00:05:37,210
affected pull requests, and the
first changed commit or commits from

81
00:05:37,250 --> 00:05:41,825
git-filter-repo's output. If the output
contained the line about orphaned LFS

82
00:05:41,865 --> 00:05:46,800
objects, mention it and attach the named
file. Support removes only sensitive data,

83
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:51,715
and only in cases where it determines that
rotating the affected credentials can't

84
00:05:51,755 --> 00:05:52,794
mitigate the risk.

85
00:05:53,134 --> 00:05:57,482
GitHub publishes neither how long it
retains unreachable commits without a

86
00:05:57,522 --> 00:06:01,752
Support-initiated garbage collection,
nor a turnaround time for Support.

87
00:06:01,792 --> 00:06:06,437
Researchers who mined force-pushed commits
observed that they appear to be kept

88
00:06:06,477 --> 00:06:10,173
indefinitely. That's their
observation, not a GitHub statement.

89
00:06:10,213 --> 00:06:14,977
The same retention is what made the recovery
of a force-pushed branch possible in

90
00:06:15,017 --> 00:06:17,349
Chapter 13: a force push doesn't delete.

91
00:06:17,689 --> 00:06:22,412
The controls. Prevention fails sometimes,
so you design for the day it does.

92
00:06:22,452 --> 00:06:27,613
Section 21B.20 lists the GitHub features
that decide how much one leaked credential

93
00:06:27,653 --> 00:06:30,809
can do and whether you can
reconstruct what it did.

94
00:06:31,149 --> 00:06:36,576
These controls reduce blast radius, which
is how far one leaked credential reaches.

95
00:06:36,616 --> 00:06:41,779
They don't prevent leaks. The textbook's
observation across the case studies of

96
00:06:41,819 --> 00:06:46,652
video 166 is sobering: the worst outcomes
involved single credentials with

97
00:06:46,692 --> 00:06:51,921
estate-wide read access, and detection
came from outsiders in nearly every case.

98
00:06:53,761 --> 00:06:59,008
Keep the analogy of video 167: the recalled
book. One reader who lends an old copy

99
00:06:59,048 --> 00:07:02,554
to the library puts the removed
page back on the shelf.

100
00:07:02,594 --> 00:07:07,776
The librarian doesn't object, because a
book was returned and that's what readers

101
00:07:07,816 --> 00:07:07,969
do.

102
00:07:08,309 --> 00:07:12,004
In Git terms: the server checks
whether a push is a fast-forward.

103
00:07:12,044 --> 00:07:16,427
It doesn't check where the new commits
came from. A merge commit that has the

104
00:07:16,467 --> 00:07:20,966
current tip as one of its parents is a
fast-forward by definition, whatever its

105
00:07:21,006 --> 00:07:22,230
other parent drags in.

106
00:07:22,570 --> 00:07:27,661
Where the analogy breaks: the reader's
copy is one object, and a stale clone is a

107
00:07:27,701 --> 00:07:32,665
graph. The clone can keep its own new
work and drop the old pages, if the owner

108
00:07:32,705 --> 00:07:35,958
replays only their own
commits onto the new history.

109
00:07:35,998 --> 00:07:37,542
That's the --onto rebase.

110
00:07:37,882 --> 00:07:41,951
The second model is for the question
"where can this data still be?"

111
00:07:41,991 --> 00:07:46,482
Count repositories, not commands. The
server, with its unreachable objects.

112
00:07:46,522 --> 00:07:51,498
The pull request refs. Each fork. Each
clone. Each of them has its own refs and its

113
00:07:51,538 --> 00:07:54,156
own objects, and each
has a different owner.

114
00:07:55,996 --> 00:08:00,648
Quick quiz. From main, is the
commit that added .env reachable?

115
00:08:00,688 --> 00:08:05,265
A, no, because the history was
rewritten. B, yes. Your answer?

116
00:08:10,105 --> 00:08:15,527
B, yes. Follow the upper parent of the
merge, back along the old history, to

117
00:08:15,567 --> 00:08:19,120
0805fd8. That's the whole
incident in one picture.

118
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:23,801
Here is the same incident as the
root-cause box of section 21B.18.

119
00:08:23,841 --> 00:08:29,376
Read the mechanism line: a pull in a stale
clone merged old and new history, and the

120
00:08:29,416 --> 00:08:30,970
push was a fast-forward.

121
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:37,324
Into the lab. Asha cloned before the
incident and has one local commit.

122
00:08:37,364 --> 00:08:40,659
She wasn't told to stop, or
didn't read the message.

123
00:08:40,699 --> 00:08:42,391
That happens on every team.

124
00:08:42,731 --> 00:08:47,513
Step 1: an ordinary morning. 🟡 CAUTION:
git pull is a fetch, a download from the

125
00:08:47,553 --> 00:08:51,130
server, followed by one integration
into the current branch.

126
00:08:51,170 --> 00:08:54,989
It moves the current branch, and
here it creates a merge commit.

127
00:08:55,329 --> 00:09:00,468
Predict two things. What will the fetch
part of the pull report about origin/main?

128
00:09:00,508 --> 00:09:03,500
And will the push need
force? Say both out loud.

129
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:12,892
Look at the two lines with a plus
sign and the words "forced update".

130
00:09:12,932 --> 00:09:18,483
That was the warning, and it scrolled past.
Then "Merge made by the 'ort' strategy".

131
00:09:18,523 --> 00:09:24,007
The graph shows the merge commit f7ed5a9
with the clean tip c8ce738 on one side and

132
00:09:24,047 --> 00:09:29,464
her commit 95f98b0 on the other. Then the
last line: the push went from c8ce738 to

133
00:09:29,504 --> 00:09:33,324
f7ed5a9 with two dots, not
three, and without a plus sign.

134
00:09:33,364 --> 00:09:38,849
A fast-forward. There's the detail from the
opening: the rule forbids force pushes,

135
00:09:38,889 --> 00:09:40,712
and nothing here was forced.

136
00:09:41,052 --> 00:09:42,587
Step 2: the server.

137
00:09:42,927 --> 00:09:48,307
Six snapshots with the key are reachable
from the server's refs again, and the

138
00:09:48,347 --> 00:09:54,144
pickaxe, a log search for one string, names
the original commit, 0805fd8, as part of

139
00:09:54,184 --> 00:09:54,491
main.

140
00:09:54,831 --> 00:09:59,104
Step 3: three ways a stale
clone meets the new history.

141
00:09:59,144 --> 00:10:05,611
Replay labs/run ch21b/stale-clone-rebase.
It builds the same situation three times.

142
00:10:05,651 --> 00:10:11,962
First: git pull --rebase as the clone's
first contact with the rewritten history.

143
00:10:12,302 --> 00:10:17,378
When run for the chapter, this replayed
only the local commit, because the pull

144
00:10:17,418 --> 00:10:22,623
still knew the previous value of origin/main.
Her commit is now 30cf503 on top of

145
00:10:22,663 --> 00:10:26,443
c8ce738, and the count is zero.
That looks like the answer.

146
00:10:26,483 --> 00:10:31,300
It isn't an instruction you can hand
out, and the second variant shows why.

147
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:36,194
Second: the clone has already fetched,
and then rebases onto origin/main.

148
00:10:36,234 --> 00:10:40,159
Predict: which commits will Git
treat as hers? Say it out loud.

149
00:10:44,999 --> 00:10:49,459
Read the error: "could not apply
0805fd8... Add staging settings".

150
00:10:49,499 --> 00:10:53,073
Git tried to replay the
commit that added the secret.

151
00:10:53,413 --> 00:10:58,686
After a plain fetch, the knowledge of the
previous origin/main is no longer used, so

152
00:10:58,726 --> 00:11:03,935
git rebase origin/main treats every old
commit that is missing from the new history

153
00:11:03,975 --> 00:11:09,248
as hers. The rebase stops in a conflict with
a detached HEAD, and git rebase --abort

154
00:11:09,288 --> 00:11:10,576
returns to the start.

155
00:11:10,916 --> 00:11:12,486
Third: the explicit form.

156
00:11:12,826 --> 00:11:17,966
The old upstream tip is read from the
reflog of main, the clone's own list of the

157
00:11:18,006 --> 00:11:22,763
positions main has had. Its oldest entry
is d4b8762, the commit she cloned.

158
00:11:23,103 --> 00:11:27,450
--onto origin/main with that old base
replays only what came after it.

159
00:11:27,490 --> 00:11:32,714
One commit, on the clean history. This is
the form to send to colleagues, because it

160
00:11:32,754 --> 00:11:34,970
works whatever the clone did before.

161
00:11:35,310 --> 00:11:41,364
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In the lab
shell, or in any clone you have, type git

162
00:11:41,404 --> 00:11:45,789
reflog show main, with your own
branch name if it's not main.

163
00:11:45,829 --> 00:11:49,199
It only reads. Find the
bottom line. I'll wait.

164
00:11:54,039 --> 00:11:58,039
That bottom line is the oldest
position your clone remembers.

165
00:11:58,079 --> 00:12:01,020
In Asha's clone it was
d4b8762, the old base.

166
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:06,755
Step 4: the tabletop, from eradication
to the end. Replay labs/run

167
00:12:06,795 --> 00:12:13,261
ch21b/lab-31-1-tabletop. The first three
snippets, confirm, contain and assess,

168
00:12:13,301 --> 00:12:19,602
belong to video 166. We start at the
fourth. First the tip is cleaned with an

169
00:12:19,642 --> 00:12:20,919
ordinary commit.

170
00:12:21,259 --> 00:12:26,341
The tip is clean and the tag still has
the key. In the tabletop, the decision to

171
00:12:26,381 --> 00:12:31,526
rewrite has been taken and recorded. The
rewrite, its verification and the forced

172
00:12:31,566 --> 00:12:36,775
push are the mechanics of video 167. So
snippets 05-rewrite, 06-verify-rewrite and

173
00:12:36,815 --> 00:12:41,448
07-force-push pass on screen without
stopping, and we stop at the server.

174
00:12:41,788 --> 00:12:46,222
On your own server you can prune, and
after the prune the object is gone.

175
00:12:46,262 --> 00:12:48,305
On GitHub only GitHub Support can.

176
00:12:48,645 --> 00:12:54,020
Step 5: prevention, and its test. The lab
installs a pre-receive hook on the server

177
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:58,195
and tests it with Ravi's clone,
which still has the old history.

178
00:12:58,535 --> 00:13:01,618
Ravi does exactly what Asha
did: pull, merge, push.

179
00:13:01,658 --> 00:13:06,639
Read the remote's answer: "push declined",
the commit named, and "pre-receive hook

180
00:13:06,679 --> 00:13:10,925
declined". The same morning routine,
and this time the server refuses.

181
00:13:11,265 --> 00:13:16,441
Step 6: the failure scenario and the
recovery. The lab then removes the guard, to

182
00:13:16,481 --> 00:13:21,784
show the state of a team that stopped before
prevention, and Asha pushes the secret

183
00:13:21,824 --> 00:13:26,484
back. Snippet 12-failure, shown briefly,
is the incident of step 1 again.

184
00:13:26,524 --> 00:13:31,700
The recovery has two halves. The server:
the cleanup clone force-pushes the clean

185
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:35,176
refs again, the guard goes
back, the server is pruned.

186
00:13:35,216 --> 00:13:36,142
Then the clone.

187
00:13:36,482 --> 00:13:41,667
🔴 DANGEROUS: git reset --hard. It discards
uncommitted changes in the working tree

188
00:13:41,707 --> 00:13:46,765
and the index. Preview with git status.
The commits it leaves behind stay in the

189
00:13:46,805 --> 00:13:51,991
reflog. Here it is appropriate because the
reflog has shown exactly which entry is

190
00:13:52,031 --> 00:13:54,030
her own commit before the merge.

191
00:13:54,370 --> 00:13:58,898
Read the reflog of main from the
bottom: clone, her commit, the merge.

192
00:13:58,938 --> 00:14:01,508
main@{1} is her commit before the merge.

193
00:14:01,848 --> 00:14:07,410
Reset to it, then replay only that commit
onto the clean history with --onto.

194
00:14:07,450 --> 00:14:11,993
The result is one new commit,
01e4fd0, on top of the clean tip.

195
00:14:12,333 --> 00:14:14,407
Step 7: verification.

196
00:14:14,747 --> 00:14:19,678
Five checks, in the order that matters.
The provider says the key is revoked.

197
00:14:19,718 --> 00:14:23,229
The server's main is the clean
history plus her commit.

198
00:14:23,269 --> 00:14:28,329
The scan over all refs prints zero. git
fsck --unreachable on the server prints

199
00:14:28,369 --> 00:14:33,751
nothing. And in each of the three clones,
asking for the first changed commit fails.

200
00:14:38,591 --> 00:14:40,239
Five mistakes to watch for.

201
00:14:40,579 --> 00:14:45,502
Telling colleagues to "pull". Root cause:
a pull in a stale clone merges old and new

202
00:14:45,542 --> 00:14:50,169
history, and the resulting push is a
fast-forward that no force-push rule sees.

203
00:14:50,509 --> 00:14:55,374
Telling colleagues to run git rebase
origin/main. Root cause: in a clone that has

204
00:14:55,414 --> 00:15:00,461
already fetched, every old commit missing
from the new history counts as local work,

205
00:15:00,501 --> 00:15:03,004
the commit that added the secret included.

206
00:15:03,344 --> 00:15:08,372
Forgetting the tags in the clones. Root
cause: tags are not updated by an ordinary

207
00:15:08,412 --> 00:15:13,193
fetch, so a clone keeps the old tag and
with it the old history until tags are

208
00:15:13,233 --> 00:15:14,553
deleted and refetched.

209
00:15:14,893 --> 00:15:19,670
Having each colleague run the same filter
command. Root cause: identical commands

210
00:15:19,710 --> 00:15:24,250
can still produce different commit IDs,
so the clones would no longer share a

211
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:25,677
history with the server.

212
00:15:26,017 --> 00:15:30,649
Treating the force push as the end on GitHub.
Root cause: pull request refs, cached

213
00:15:30,689 --> 00:15:35,321
views, forks and unreachable objects are
outside the reach of your Git commands and

214
00:15:35,361 --> 00:15:36,334
