On Monday the file was back.

218
00:15:50,895 --> 00:15:55,801
The lead didn't ask who had force-pushed,
because the branch rule would have blocked

219
00:15:55,841 --> 00:15:55,978
it.

220
00:15:56,318 --> 00:16:00,895
She ran git log --merges -1 on main and
looked at the parents of the newest merge

221
00:16:00,935 --> 00:16:05,228
commit: one was the rewritten tip, the
other descended from the old history.

222
00:16:05,268 --> 00:16:08,648
The author of the merge had been
on leave during the freeze.

223
00:16:08,988 --> 00:16:14,002
The fix followed the root-cause box. The
clean refs were force-pushed again from the

224
00:16:14,042 --> 00:16:17,431
cleanup clone, which had been
kept for exactly this case.

225
00:16:17,471 --> 00:16:22,063
The colleague's clone was repaired with
the --onto form, so that only his two

226
00:16:22,103 --> 00:16:26,996
commits were replayed. And two controls
were added: a push ruleset that blocks the

227
00:16:27,036 --> 00:16:31,929
file path, and a line in the freeze
announcement that names the per-clone sequence

228
00:16:31,969 --> 00:16:33,554
instead of the word "pull".

229
00:16:33,894 --> 00:16:38,130
The Support request for the pull request
refs and the cached views, which had been

230
00:16:38,170 --> 00:16:42,198
filed on Friday with the count of affected
pull requests and the first changed

231
00:16:42,238 --> 00:16:43,450
commit, wasn't affected.

