249
00:18:07,953 --> 00:18:11,142
Let's land this. Say each
of these in your own words.

250
00:18:11,482 --> 00:18:16,037
After a rewrite, every clone that still
has the old history can return it with an

251
00:18:16,077 --> 00:18:20,292
ordinary pull and push, because the
merge is a fast-forward for the server.

252
00:18:20,632 --> 00:18:25,747
The safe instruction is: re-clone; or
delete and refetch tags, rebase with --onto

253
00:18:25,787 --> 00:18:31,029
from the old upstream tip, then expire and
prune, and verify that the first changed

254
00:18:31,069 --> 00:18:31,984
commit is gone.

255
00:18:32,324 --> 00:18:37,950
git pull --rebase works only as the first
contact; after a fetch, a plain rebase

256
00:18:37,990 --> 00:18:41,421
onto origin/main tries to
replay the old history.

257
00:18:41,761 --> 00:18:46,460
On GitHub, unreachable commits, pull
request refs, forks and clones survive the

258
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:51,019
force push; Support can act on the first
two, and only where rotation cannot

259
00:18:51,059 --> 00:18:52,099
mitigate the risk.

260
00:18:52,439 --> 00:18:56,522
Governance controls do not prevent leaks;
they limit what a leaked credential

261
00:18:56,562 --> 00:18:58,611
reaches and let you answer what it did.

